**Question #6:**

sort(scores) and scores[order(scores)] produce the same final values, but they work completely differently under the hood.

In your own words, explain what order(scores) actually returns (look at the raw numbers it gives you), and why that output is useless on its own unless you use it to index something.

**Answer #6:**

order(scores) just outputs a list of index numbers, like 5 7 1 3 6 2 4. It does not tell you what the actual scores or names are, it just tells which row/position to look at first, second, third, and so on. Without indexing or putting it on a [], it's just a random looking numbers that don't give you an actual dataset.

**Question #7:**

Explain why sort() alone could NOT have been used to reorder the whole students data frame by score (try to imagine running sort(students) and think about what “sorting a table” would even mean).

Why is order() the correct tool whenever you need to keep multiple related columns lined up with each other?

**Answer #7:**

sort() only works on one vector at a time. If you try to run sort(students) on a whole dataframe, R won't know which column you want to sort by, and it can't sort an entire table directly without breaking the rows apart.

order() is the right tool because it returns the row index sequence based on a specific column. When you put that index sequence in the row position of df[order(...), ], R moves the whole rows together so all the columns stay properly aligned.

**Question #8:**

#PART A

```{r}
cbind(scores = scores, rank = rank(scores))
```
**Answer #8:**

**a.** What rank did each of them receive?

Ben and Dex both got a rank of 6.5

**b.** Ben and Dex both scored 92, the highest score in the class, but under the default rank() they did NOT both simply get rank “1” or rank “7” as a whole number. Explain exactly what value they received by default and why R computes it that way for tied values.

Ben and Dex are tied for the highest score (92), which takes up positions 6 and 7 in the sorted order. Since R defaults to averaging tied ranks (ties.method = "average"), it adds 6 and 7 together and divides by 2 to give both of them 6.5.

